= Editorial protocol =

{{Source status|status=source-grounded operating method|note=Public-safe distillation of recurring BITwiki research and Book Matter working rules.}}

BITwiki canonicalization follows a research-and-revision process rather than one-pass rewriting.

== Loop ==
<pre>
RAW SIGNAL
→ CONTEXT / SCOPE
→ RESEARCH
→ MULTIPLE LENSES / PARTNERS
→ MODEL / STRUCTURE
→ DISTILL SIGNAL
→ REVISE
→ CROSS-REVIEW
→ VERIFY
→ CANONICALIZE
→ TRANSCLUDE / RELATE
→ RECUR
</pre>

== Rules ==
* Verify state and source before acting.
* Preserve source text before interpreting it.
* Work within explicit scope.
* Use multiple perspectives or reviewers when material is consequential or ambiguous.
* Do not accept the first revision merely because it is coherent.
* Prefer slow, surgical, intelligent small wins over rash multi-component changes.
* Distinguish historical design from current implementation.
* Preserve citations and provenance through moves, merges, rewrites, and transclusion.
* Mark uncertainty, contradiction, speculation, and unresolved source gaps.
* Preserve sophisticated distinctions while removing accidental complexity.
* Optimize for transposability, modularity, composability, depth, nuance, and sophistication.

== Book Matter extraction and editorial reuse ==

[[BITwiki:Book Matter]] describes the intellectual role of material; [[BITwiki:Transclusion]] documents the composition model. Neither vocabulary membership nor section length is enough to justify extracting a separate page. Decisions belong in this editorial protocol:

# '''Establish the source:''' identify the existing article, its authoritative claims, sources, revision and maintained context; do not mistake a copied passage for independent authority.
# '''Demonstrate the need:''' name actual consuming pages, independent review responsibilities, or a verified repeated retrieval requirement. If no real reuse exists, prefer an inline section or link.
# '''Choose the boundary:''' keep limitations, competing interpretations, evidentiary caveats, citations and definitions needed to interpret the component.
# '''Assign ownership:''' identify the canonical source and the reviewers responsible for maintenance; document affected host pages or other dependencies.
# '''Choose the appropriate primitive:''' ordinary article prose, hyperlink, core transclusion, Template, or verified selective-section extension. Do not assume that Main slash titles enable subpage features.
# '''Review both contexts:''' inspect the source and every substantive host, including semantic assertions. An annotation about a fragment must not accidentally become an assertion about its host.
# '''Validate in layers:''' repository tests cover source contracts; only live parser/SMW inspection can demonstrate production rendering and semantic behavior.
# '''Maintain and revise:''' track affected hosts when changing a shared source; preserve version history and attribution through extraction, relocation or retirement.

Use [[BITwiki:Page format]] for reader-facing structure and [[Help:Transclusion]] for actual wikitext operations. A static composition preview is not evidence that a component registry, retrieval service or production transclusion is deployed.

== Inquiry, methods, and accountable authorship ==
BITwiki is a curated research environment, not an authority that establishes truth by publication. The method of evaluation follows the question: empirical observation, formal proof, documented history, engineering testing and interpretation have different evidentiary jobs. Credentials, reputation and peer review may inform source assessment but cannot replace examination of the work itself. See [[BITwiki:Epistemics]], [[BITwiki:Sources]] and [[BITwiki:Claim validation]].

Human and artificial contributors can research, draft, challenge and revise within their assigned permissions. For consequential material, preserve enough provenance to distinguish originating source, participant direction, machine-generated transformation, validation and editorial admission. The historical research-space Charter proposed a specific agent-mediated route through BIThub; that is lineage, not a permanent requirement for every contribution channel. See [[BITwiki:Contributing]] and [[BITwiki:Human-AI symbiosis]].

== Canonicalization is not paraphrasing ==
For important authored passages recover source, date/version, exact wording, later revisions, unique meaning, citations, and context. New prose must not silently replace source-derived prose.

